// ============================================================================
// unified.z  --  one declaration form for module / struct / func / sum / shape
// ============================================================================
//
// THE RULE
//
//     Name <compile-time args> (runtime args) -> projection { body }
//
// Every declaration takes args and builds a block of named values.
// The projection says what escapes the block:
//
//     -> ()        nothing escapes            procedure
//     -> T         one value escapes          function
//     -> &T        a LOCATION escapes         a writable element
//     -> self      all of the block           struct / module / shape
//     -> {a, b}    these members of it        multiple return, AND visibility
//     -> oneof     one member of it, tagged   sum
//
// APPLYING A DECLARATION. Stated because everything else depends on it:
//
//     List           the declaration
//     List<u8>       ...compile-time args applied   ->  a TYPE
//     List<u8>(mem)  ...runtime args applied        ->  a VALUE
//
//     No runtime group at all (`x :: -> T {...}`) means the thing IS its
//     value, already evaluated. An explicit empty `()` means you call it.
//     That is the whole of why `Heap()` is a call and `Writer` is not.
//
// BRACKETS
//     <>   compile-time args      List<u8>, ptr<T>
//     ()   runtime args           Heap()
//     []   indexing               xs[0]
//
// BINDING.  `:` is type ascription and nothing else. The binder is what
// carries meaning, and a hole is the absence of one.
//
//     name          :: v     compile-time binding
//     name : T      :: v     ...with the type spelled out
//     name          := v     runtime binding
//     name : T      := v     ...with the type spelled out
//     name : T               no binder  ->  HOLE
//     name          := undefined        a field you fill in later
//
//     `=` is assignment: mutating an existing binding, filling a declared
//     hole, a default in a parameter list, or passing a compile-time arg by
//     name (`to_string<F = hex_u8>`). It never creates a binding.
//
//     SO THE PROJECTION DECIDES WHICH ONE A MEMBER TAKES, and this is worth
//     stating because the three spellings look like one act:
//
//         -> self      you are building a FRESH block. Nothing exists yet, so
//                      every member is `::` or `:=`.
//         -> SomeType  you are building a value of a type that ALREADY HAS
//                      HOLES. Members matching those holes are `=`; anything
//                      else is a fresh, private member and takes `::`.
//
//     Which is why `Heap` writes `alloc :: ...` and `hex_u8` writes
//     `format = ...` for what looks like the same act. Heap says `-> self` and
//     satisfies Allocator STRUCTURALLY -- it never names it, so there is no
//     hole in scope and `alloc` is created. A policy instance must NAME its
//     type in the projection, because that is how the solver finds it, so the
//     holes are in scope and it fills one. The binder is the structural /
//     nominal difference made visible. See glossary.z.
//
//     A runtime param of a `-> self` def IS a field: the block is what you
//     kept, so the params are in it. A def whose body would be empty may drop
//     it -- `Span :: (lo: u32, hi: u32) -> self` is a record declaration.
//     A field bound with `::` costs zero bytes; it lives in the type.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HOLES.  `name : T` with no binder is a hole. What a hole MEANS is decided by
// the projection, and that single fact is the whole of both sums and
// interfaces:
//
//     -> self    CONJUNCTIVE. All holes must be filled to instantiate. A block
//                with an unfilled hole cannot be instantiated, so it is usable
//                only as a bound or behind a pointer. That is all an interface
//                ever was, and it applies to every block equally -- a List with
//                one unfilled field is abstract for the same reason Allocator
//                is. Filling the holes gives you a value.
//
//     -> oneof   DISJUNCTIVE. Exactly one hole is filled. A sum is never
//                abstract; there is nothing left to fill.
//
// So `Allocator` and `Option` are the same declaration shape read two ways.
// THERE IS NO `trait`, `interface`, `abstract`, `enum` OR `union`.
//
// The projection quantifies over HOLES and nothing else. `::` and `:=` members
// are unaffected by it, so a sum carries methods exactly as a struct does:
//
//                       -> self                    -> oneof
//     name : T          requirement                variant
//     name :: v         method / ct field          method
//     name := v         runtime field              -- (no shared storage)
//
// A HOLE MAY BE A TYPE, because `type` is an ordinary type whose values are
// types, and a hole is a member you did not supply:
//
//     Iterator :: -> self {
//         Item: type                          // supplied by the implementer
//         next: (&self) -> Option<self.Item>
//     }
//
// That is the whole of what other languages call an associated type. It is
// filled like any other member -- `Item :: T` -- and it is the difference
// between a thing the CALLER chooses (a parameter, searched on) and a thing
// the INSTANCE decides (a hole, reported back). See NOTE 9. `type` is a
// built-in type name and not a keyword, exactly as `u32` and `bool` are.
//
// Construction needs no new rule: `=` already fills a declared hole in a
// literal, so `Option<u8>{ some = 3 }`. Since a variant is a hole of type T,
// the variant name is also a derived function (T) -> Self: `Option<u8>.some(3)`.
//
// `-> oneof {a, b}` is a sum over a subset -- variants only the declaring
// module can construct, matchable by everyone. It falls out of visibility
// below rather than being designed.
//
// On the name: `-> T` is already glossed "one value escapes", so `-> one`
// would collide with the projection above it, and a bare `one` in a slot that
// otherwise holds type names burns a plausible identifier. `union` is poisoned
// by C, `variant` names a member rather than the whole, `either` implies two.
// It is spelled bare and not `-> oneof self` because a sum does not project the
// whole block -- one member escapes, which puts it in `{a, b}`'s family.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// VISIBILITY IS THE PROJECTION. `-> self` exports the whole block; `-> {a, b}`
// exports two names. There is no `pub` and no export list, because the
// projection always was one. See `io` below.
//
// A value may escape while its type does not. `io.out` is exported and
// `FileWriter` is not, which is an opaque type for free.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PLACES.  `-> &T` says A LOCATION ESCAPES, and it is what lets a container
// hand out an element you can write to:
//
//     [] :: (&self, i: u32) -> &T { self.items[i] }
//
//     v := xs[i]        read it
//     xs[i] = v         write it
//     xs[i] += 1        both, and `[]` is called exactly once
//     p := &xs[i]       take its address, which is a ptr<T>
//
// `&` is the right symbol because it already means "the thing itself, not a
// copy" wherever it appears -- `&self` is the actual block, `&x` is the actual
// x. In a projection it means the same.
//
// FOUR RULES, and they are the whole of it:
//
//   1. `&T` APPEARS IN TWO POSITIONS, BOTH OF THEM CALLING CONVENTIONS: after
//      `->`, and in a parameter list. It NEVER appears inside another type.
//      There is no `Option<&V>`, no `List<&T>`, no `&T` field. That is what
//      stops it from quietly becoming a second pointer type -- and it is also
//      what makes the parameter form worth having, below.
//
//   2. THE BODY MUST EVALUATE TO A PLACE, not to a pointer: a field, an index
//      into a pointer, a deref, or another `-> &T` call. Symmetric with
//      everything else -- `-> T`'s body is a value, `-> self`'s body is the
//      block, `-> &T`'s body is a location.
//
//   3. UNDERNEATH IT IS `ptr<T>`, exactly as `&self` is `ptr<self>` underneath.
//      The call site derefs. No new representation and nothing in the ABI.
//
//   4. `xs[i] += 1` CALLS THE ACCESSOR ONCE. Stated because accessors can have
//      side effects, and because C++ gets this wrong with proxy objects.
//
// WHY NOT `-> ptr<T>` AND AN EXPLICIT DEREF (`xs[i].* = v`), which would have
// cost no syntax at all: it makes `xs[i]` -- the shortest and commonest
// spelling -- mean the ADDRESS of the element, when every reader alive reads it as the element.
// `v := xs[i]` would compile and hand back a pointer. The bare form has to be
// the ordinary meaning; the unusual one is what you type extra characters for.
//
// WHAT IT DELIBERATELY WILL NOT DO. A packed container -- a bitset, a
// compressed array, an element computed on the fly -- has no location to hand
// back, so it cannot offer `[]` as a place at all. It offers `get(i)` and
// `set(i, b)`. There are no proxy objects, which is the disease `vector<bool>`
// died of. And a fallible lookup is not a place, because there is no such
// thing as "maybe a location": `Map.get` returns `Option<ptr<V>>` and the
// match arm derefs it.
//
// A PARAMETER MAY BE A PLACE, and this is not a new idea -- `&self` has been
// one since the first draft. `self.count += 1` writes through the caller's
// value with nothing spelled out, so a general `(out: &u32)` is `&self`
// without the special case, and forbidding it was the asymmetry.
//
//     tally :: (out: &u32) (k: slice<u8>, v: Json) -> () { out += 1 }
//
// The call site is unchanged -- `sort(&xs)`, an argument is still explicit.
// What changes is what the SIGNATURE promises, and it promises something the
// language could not say before:
//
//     print :: (w: &World) -> ()        I will not keep this
//     keep  :: (w: ptr<World>) -> ()    I might store this
//
// A `&T` parameter provably cannot be stored, because rule 1 forbids the field
// that would hold it. Both spellings below are in this file and the difference
// is real: `sort` borrows a list for the length of a call, `ListIter` keeps
// one, and now they do not look alike.
//
//     sort     :: <T, O: Ord<T> = ?> (xs: &List<T, _>) -> ()
//     ListIter :: <T, M> (xs: ptr<List<T, M>>) -> self      // a FIELD
//
// It is a down payment on the mutability question in OPEN and not an answer to
// it: it says who may KEEP a pointer, and still nothing about who may write
// through one, or for how long it stays valid.
//
// A SHAPE NAME IS ALREADY A POINTER in a parameter list -- `(m: Allocator)` is
// the erased one -- so `&Shape` would say nothing new and is not written.
//
// WHAT IT BUYS, beyond writing to an element: it is what makes `-> {a, b}`
// usable on a container. Without places, every caller reaches around `[]` into
// the raw `items` field, so closing the projection breaks every program that
// touches a List. With them, `List` below exports six names and hides its
// storage, and nothing outside it can see a pointer.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// KEYWORDS.  `self` is the only new one in the declaration form. It always
// means "the block".
//     -> self               in a projection: all of the block escapes
//     self                  a receiver, by value
//     &self                 a receiver, by pointer
//     ptr<self>             what &self desugars to
//     -> Result<self, E>    a projection may WRAP self: fallible construction
//
// WHEN `self` NEEDS A RECEIVER, AND WHEN IT DOES NOT. It is one meaning; the
// receiver exists only to say WHICH instance, and is therefore required
// exactly when the block is a type rather than a value:
//
//     block has a runtime group   ->  it is a TYPE. Many values exist, so a
//                                     member must take `self` / `&self` to
//                                     know which one it was called on.
//                                     `List`, `Heap()`, `Arena`.
//
//     block has no runtime group  ->  it IS its value, already evaluated.
//                                     There is exactly one, so `self` is
//                                     available lexically with nothing passed.
//                                     `mem`, `io`, `reverse_u8`, and every
//                                     policy instance.
//
// The second case is the one that lets an instance recurse into itself -- see
// `Formattable<u8>` below and NOTE 7. It also answers a question the earlier
// draft left open: yes, a member of `mem` may say `self`, and it means `mem`.
//
// A member of a block that HAS a runtime group may not say bare `self`; that
// is an error asking for `&self`, not an implicit receiver.
//
// The rest of the budget, written down so it stops growing by accident:
//     oneof                 a projection
//     match  _              destructuring
//     try                   propagate the `err` arm of a Result
//     defer                 run at scope exit
//     if while for loop return continue break
//
// There is no `struct`, `enum`, `union`, `interface`, `trait`, `impl`,
// `operator`, `pub`, `dyn`, or `import`. Imports are ordinary compile-time
// bindings of an ordinary compile-time function: `std :: import("std")`.
//
// MATCH.  An arm is spelled like a declaration -- a name, a runtime group
// binding the payload, a body. Nothing new to learn.
//
//     match (v) {
//         none     { ... }
//         some (x) { ... }
//         _        { ... }
//     }
//
// `_` is a hole in a BINDING position: a wildcard arm, an ignored payload, or
// a compile-time arg you decline to name (`ptr<List<T, _>>`). One symbol, one
// idea. DISCARDING A RESULT is the fourth use of the same symbol, and the
// rule that goes with it: a value may not be dropped silently.
//
//     xs.push(v)              fine -- push projects `()`, there is no value
//     xs.pop()                an error: an Option<T> went nowhere
//     _ := xs.pop()           "bind it to nothing", said out loud
//
// The rule exists because `-> Result<T, E>` is the language's error channel,
// and an ignored Result is the one mistake it must not make easy to write.
//
// MATCH IS AN EXPRESSION, and this is what keeps sum types from turning code
// into a pyramid. A one-arm-plus-`_` match BINDS instead of nesting:
//
//     obj  := match (doc)         { object (o) { o }  _ { return } }
//     tags := match (obj["tags"]) { some   (t) { t }  _ { return } }
//     xs   := match (tags)        { array  (a) { a }  _ { return } }
//
// Each line either produces a value or leaves. Nested matches are for when the
// failure arms do real work; when they do not, bind and move on. Languages
// that miss this end up adding `if let`, `guard let` and `let else` as three
// separate forms.
//
// OPERATORS. There is no `operator` keyword, and there are two kinds of
// operator, because the shape/policy test asked of them gives two answers:
//
//     [] ()            take self  ->  MEMBERS of the block
//     + - * / == <     take none  ->  POLICIES, found by the solver
//
//     [] :: (&self, i: u32) -> T       indexing
//     () :: (&self, v: u8)  -> ()      calling
//
//     a + b    ->  Add<A, B>.add(a, b)
//     a * b    ->  Mul<A, B>.mul(a, b)
//     a % b    ->  Rem<A, B>.rem(a, b)
//     a == b   ->  Eq<A, B>.eq(a, b)
//     a < b    ->  Ord<A>.compare(a, b) is negative
//     a += b   ->  a = a + b
//     -a       ->  Neg<A>.neg(a)
//
// Ask NOTE 1's question of each. Two ways for a List to index itself? No --
// indexing is a property of the container, so `[]` is a member. Two ways to
// add two vectors, or to compare two u8? Yes -- saturating and wrapping,
// ascending and descending -- so those are choices the caller makes, and a
// choice the caller makes is a policy. See std.num and NOTE 6.
//
// THE DESUGARING BOTTOMS OUT IN THE PRIMITIVE INSTANCES, and it has to be
// written so that it does. `a < b` expands to a test of `Ord<A>.compare`'s
// i32 against zero as an INSTRUCTION, not to another `<` -- otherwise the
// expansion is infinite -- and for the same reason the pooled `Ord<u8>` below
// is written in intrinsics rather than in `<`. A primitive instance is where
// arithmetic stops being a call, so there is no arithmetic inside one.
//
// An operator always names the SOLVER'S DEFAULT for its operand types. To use
// a different instance, name it and call its member -- `saturating.add(a, b)`.
// A generic function that wants the choice threaded through takes the policy
// as a `= ?` parameter and calls it by hand, exactly as `sort` does with Ord.
// The consequence, stated because it is a real decision: a generic body's bare
// `a + b` is checked when the function is INSTANTIATED, not where it is
// declared. Declaring the bound is how you get the earlier error.
//
// `&&` and `||` are not policies and never will be: they must not evaluate
// their right operand, so they are control flow wearing an operator's clothes.
//
// A closure is therefore not a special form. It is a block with a `()` member,
// which is why `Fn<A, R>` below is an ordinary shape and needs no function-type
// machinery. See NOTE 6.
//
// POINTERS
//     ptr<T>       typed
//     ptr          the same thing with its arg defaulted to opaque. Not a
//                  special form -- compile-time args take defaults anyway.
//
// REACHING THROUGH A POINTER. The compiler inserts a deref and an address-of
// in a few positions and never said which, so: it is these three, and nothing
// else is implicit.
//
//     p.field       `.` reaches through ANY number of pointers. So does a
//     p[i]          symbolic member -- `p[i]` is `p.[](i)` and reaches too.
//     xs.push(v)    a `&self` receiver takes the address of the value it was
//                   called on. `sort(&xs)` does NOT: an argument is explicit.
//
//     p.*           REACH THROUGH AND STOP. The whole pointee, as a place.
//
// `.*` is the shape of the rule, not decoration: `.` reaches through and stops
// at a member, `.*` reaches through and stops at the value. The two spellings
// are the same act ending in different places, which is why the deref lives
// after the dot rather than being its own symbol.
//
//     out.* += 1              write the whole pointee
//     v.* = Vec2(1.0, 2.0)    ...even when it is a struct
//     a.b.*                   postfix, so it chains left to right
//
// `.*` is available BECAUSE `*` is not a member name. `[]` and `()` are, so
// `f.()(x)` and `a.[](i)` are real spellings; had arithmetic stayed a member
// this deref would have collided with the multiply member on the first day.
//
// AND `^` IS FREE FOR XOR, which is the reason it is not the deref. A hash
// function writes `h = (h ^ k[i]) * prime`, and a language whose deref is also
// postfix `^` eventually has to parse `h^ ^ x`. Bitwise and (`&`) has the same
// problem against address-of; deref was the half that could move.
//
// DEREF IS NEVER OPTIONAL, and this is why full auto-deref is not on offer:
//     p = q         make the two pointers equal
//     p.* = q.*     copy the pointee
// Both are things you have to be able to say, and they must not be one text.
//
// COMPILE-TIME ARGS COME IN THREE KINDS
//     <T>                      a TYPE, inferred from a runtime argument's type
//     <F: Formattable<T> = ?>  a POLICY, solved by search: nothing at the call
//                              site mentions it, so the solver goes and finds it
//     <indent: u32 = 2>        a VALUE. An ordinary compile-time binding in the
//                              parameter list; `capacity :: 10` is the same
//                              thing as a member.
//     Any of them may be passed by name at the call site with `=`, and once a
//     declaration mixes an inferred type with a value there is no other way to
//     call it: `dump<indent = 4>` cannot be written positionally, because the
//     type parameter in front of it is never spelled.
//
// A DECLARATION WITH NO NAME is what the solver searches. `::` with nothing
// on its left is an anonymous compile-time binding; it goes in the pool.
// Give it a name and it is reachable only by hand.
//
// A TYPE IN VALUE POSITION IS THE SOLVER'S DEFAULT INSTANCE. `Ord<u8>.compare`
// means "whatever instance the solver picks for Ord<u8>". This is not sugar --
// it is what lets a derived instance recurse into itself, and what a named
// instance calls to delegate. See `reverse_u8`.
//
// COHERENCE. There is EXACTLY ONE anonymous instance per (policy, type) in a
// program, and the pool is GLOBAL -- not scoped to what a module imported.
// A second one, or one that overlaps an existing one, is an error reported
// against the two declarations. There is no priority order, no most-specific-
// wins and no specialisation.
//
// The pool has to be global because the alternative is that a type's identity
// depends on who imported what: `Map<slice<u8>, V>` picks its `Key` from the
// pool, so an import-scoped pool would let two modules build two different
// maps with the same spelling. NOTE 4 already refuses that hazard for UFCS
// and shape satisfaction; this is the same refusal.
//
// THERE IS NO ORPHAN RULE. Anyone may write an instance for anyone's type, and
// that is the entire reason policies are worth having over traits. What
// replaces it is a convention with teeth: an ANONYMOUS instance is a claim on
// the global default, so publish one only for a policy or a type you own. If
// two libraries both claim `Formattable<Vec2>`, the program does not build
// until one of them names its instance -- which is the same one-word fix that
// makes `reverse_u8` reachable only by hand. See NOTE 8.
//
// ORDER INDEPENDENCE. Declarations in a block, and blocks in a file, are
// order-independent. Recursive types force it (`Slot` holds an `Entry`
// declared below it), the solver forces it (it must have seen every anonymous
// declaration before it can pick), and coherence forces it (a duplicate is a
// whole-program property). This was relied on from the first draft and never
// said.
//
// UFCS.  `a.f(x)` falls back to `f(a, x)` when `a` has no member `f`, and `f`
// is found by ORDINARY NAME LOOKUP IN THE CALLER'S SCOPE. That is precisely
// why an extrinsic function may never satisfy a shape -- see NOTE 4.
//
// TWO KINDS OF ABSTRACTION, ONE SYNTAX. See NOTE 1.
//     holes take self      ->  SHAPE.      Intrinsic to a type, matched
//                              structurally, coercible to ptr<Shape>.
//     holes do not         ->  POLICY.     Extrinsic, several may exist, the
//                              caller picks, the solver defaults it.
//     Mixing the two in one block is an error, not a third thing.
//     The test reads VALUE holes only. A `: type` hole takes no self and is
//     neither; both kinds of block may carry one.
//
// A SHAPE NAME IN AN ARGUMENT POSITION says which dispatch you are buying:
//     <M: Allocator>   a bound.   Monomorphised, calls direct, 0-byte field.
//     (m: Allocator)   ptr<Allocator>. Erased, calls indirect.
// The bracket already separates compile time from run time; it separates
// static from dynamic for the same reason, and no `dyn` is needed.
//
// ============================================================================


// ============================================================================
// std.core
// ============================================================================

Option :: <T> -> oneof {
    none: ()
    some: T
}

Result :: <T, E> -> oneof {
    ok:  T
    err: E
}

// `try e` yields the `ok` payload, or returns the `err` arm to the caller's
// projection. It is the only reason `Result` is worth having over a match.

// A closure is a block with a `()` member, so a callable is an ordinary shape.
// One per arity is the cost; see OPEN.
Fn  :: <A, R>    -> self { (): (&self, a: A)       -> R }
Fn2 :: <A, B, R> -> self { (): (&self, a: A, b: B) -> R }


// ============================================================================
// std.num  --  the operator policies
// ============================================================================
//
// One policy per operator, each with TWO operand types and an output type it
// decides for itself. Both halves of that are forced by the case that made
// operators policies in the first place:
//
//     v * 2.0        Vec2 and f32 -- so the operands may differ
//     Vec2           -- and the result matches neither of them positionally
//
// `Out` is a HOLE and not a third parameter, and the difference is the whole
// argument for type holes: the solver searches on a policy's PARAMETERS, so a
// third parameter would have to be known at the call site before the instance
// that determines it could be found. An output must not be a search key.
// See NOTE 9.

Add :: <A, B> -> self { Out: type;  add: (a: A, b: B) -> self.Out }
Sub :: <A, B> -> self { Out: type;  sub: (a: A, b: B) -> self.Out }
Mul :: <A, B> -> self { Out: type;  mul: (a: A, b: B) -> self.Out }
Div :: <A, B> -> self { Out: type;  div: (a: A, b: B) -> self.Out }
Rem :: <A, B> -> self { Out: type;  rem: (a: A, b: B) -> self.Out }
Neg :: <A>    -> self { Out: type;  neg: (a: A)       -> self.Out }

// The primitives are ordinary pooled instances. Arithmetic is not built in;
// what is built in is the instruction each of these bodies is.
:: -> Add<u8, u8> { Out :: u8;  add = (a, b) -> u8 { intrinsic_add_u8(a, b) } }
:: -> Add<f32, f32> { Out :: f32;  add = (a, b) -> f32 { intrinsic_add_f32(a, b) } }
:: -> Mul<f32, f32> { Out :: f32;  mul = (a, b) -> f32 { intrinsic_mul_f32(a, b) } }
// ...and so on, one per (operator, primitive pair). Generated, not written.

// A user type joins in by publishing instances. Nothing is added to Vec2
// itself, which is what makes `2.0 * v` possible at all -- the instance for
// the flipped operand order is just another declaration, and it does not have
// to live near f32 or belong to anyone.
Vec2 :: (x: f32, y: f32) -> self

:: -> Add<Vec2, Vec2> { Out :: Vec2;  add = (a, b) -> Vec2 { Vec2(a.x + b.x, a.y + b.y) } }
:: -> Mul<Vec2, f32>  { Out :: Vec2;  mul = (v, s) -> Vec2 { Vec2(v.x * s, v.y * s)     } }
:: -> Mul<f32, Vec2>  { Out :: Vec2;  mul = (s, v) -> Vec2 { v * s                      } }

:: -> Eq<Vec2, Vec2> { eq = (a, b) -> bool { a.x == b.x && a.y == b.y } }

// A second addition, named, therefore not in the pool. `a + b` never means
// this one; `saturating.add(a, b)` does. This is the two-of-them that makes
// arithmetic a policy rather than a member.
saturating :: -> Add<Vec2, Vec2> {
    Out :: Vec2
    add = (a, b) -> Vec2 { Vec2(clamp(a.x + b.x), clamp(a.y + b.y)) }
}


// ============================================================================
// std.mem
// ============================================================================

// Nothing but holes -- two names with types and no binder. Uninstantiable,
// therefore usable only as a bound or behind a pointer.
Allocator :: -> self {
    alloc: (&self, n: u32) -> ptr
    free:  (&self, p: ptr) -> ()
}

// The typed layer is an ordinary module of free functions. It is not part of
// the shape, so anyone can extend it, and UFCS makes it read like a method.
mem :: -> self {
    make :: <T, M: Allocator> (m: M, n: u32) -> ptr<T> { m.alloc(n * size_of<T>()) }
    drop :: <T, M: Allocator> (m: M, p: ptr<T>) -> ()  { m.free(p) }
}

// No fields -> zero-sized -> the field costs 0 bytes wherever it is stored
// and every call through it is direct.
Heap :: () -> self {
    alloc :: (&self, n: u32) -> ptr { c_malloc(n) }
    free  :: (&self, p: ptr) -> ()  { c_free(p)   }
}

Arena :: (base: ptr<u8>, size: u32) -> self {
    top := 0
    alloc :: (&self, n: u32) -> ptr {
        p := self.base + self.top
        self.top += n
        p
    }
    free :: (&self, p: ptr) -> () { }
}

// Wraps any allocator, and is itself one.
Tracking :: <M: Allocator> (inner: M) -> self {
    alloc :: (&self, n: u32) -> ptr { log("alloc %", n); self.inner.alloc(n) }
    free  :: (&self, p: ptr) -> ()  { log("free");       self.inner.free(p)  }
}


// ============================================================================
// std.io  --  the projection is the export list
// ============================================================================

Writer :: -> self {
    write_bytes: (&self, p: ptr<u8>, n: u32) -> ()
}

io :: -> {out, err, write} {

    // Not in the projection, so it does not escape. `out` does, which makes
    // its type opaque outside this block at no cost.
    FileWriter :: (fd: i32) -> self {
        write_bytes :: (&self, p: ptr<u8>, n: u32) -> () { c_write(self.fd, p, n) }
    }

    out :: FileWriter(1)
    err :: FileWriter(2)

    write :: <W: Writer> (w: W, s: slice<u8>) -> () { w.write_bytes(s.ptr, s.len) }
}


// ============================================================================
// std.list  --  methods live in the block. See NOTE 4.
// ============================================================================

// `M: Allocator` means "any type structurally providing that shape".
// ptr<Allocator> provides it too -- that is the entire static/dynamic switch,
// with no keyword. See NOTE 2.
// THE PROJECTION IS CLOSED, which is only possible because of places: `items`
// and `capacity` do not escape, so no caller can see the storage, and `[]`
// hands out a location rather than a copy. `span` exists for the same reason
// -- building a slice needs the base pointer, and closing the projection means
// asking for it by name instead of reaching in.
List :: <T, M: Allocator> (mem: M) -> {push, pop, count, [], span, deinit} {
    capacity := 10
    count    := 0

    // A field initialiser sees the runtime params directly; methods reach them
    // through `self`. `make` itself is found by lookup in this scope, per UFCS.
    items    := mem.make<T>(capacity)     // UFCS -> make<T>(mem, capacity)

    push :: (&self, item: T) -> () {
        if (self.count + 1 > self.capacity) { self.expand() }
        self.items[self.count] = item
        self.count += 1
    }

    // Returning `T` was a lie about the empty list. This is what `-> oneof`
    // buys in the first ten lines of a standard library.
    pop :: (&self) -> Option<T> {
        if (self.count == 0) { return Option<T>.none() }
        self.count -= 1
        Option<T>.some(self.items[self.count])
    }

    expand :: (&self) -> () {
        new_cap   := self.capacity * 2
        new_items := self.mem.make<T>(new_cap)
        for (i := 0; i < self.count; i += 1) { new_items[i] = self.items[i] }
        self.mem.drop(self.items)
        self.items    = new_items
        self.capacity = new_cap
    }

    deinit :: (&self) -> () {
        self.mem.drop(self.items)
        self.items = nil
        self.count = 0
    }

    // A LOCATION escapes, so `xs[i] = v` and `xs[i] += 1` are writable and
    // `&xs[i]` is a ptr<T>. The body is a place, not a pointer -- no `&`.
    [] :: (&self, i: u32) -> &T { self.items[i] }

    span :: (&self) -> slice<T> { slice(self.items, self.count) }
}


// ============================================================================
// std.iter  --  what a type hole is for
// ============================================================================
//
// `Item` is decided by the implementer and reported back, so it is a hole. The
// element type of an iterator was never a choice the caller makes -- it is a
// property of the iterator -- and threading it through as a parameter is what
// made `fold`'s signature carry a `T` that nobody at the call site chooses.

Iterator :: -> self {
    Item: type
    next: (&self) -> Option<self.Item>
}

ListIter :: <T, M> (xs: ptr<List<T, M>>) -> self {
    Item :: T                                  // fills the hole; costs 0 bytes
    i := 0
    next :: (&self) -> Option<T> {
        if (self.i >= self.xs.count) { return Option<T>.none() }
        v := self.xs[self.i]
        self.i += 1
        Option<T>.some(v)
    }
}

// A compile-time member of a BOUND is reached the same way as one of a value:
// `I.Item`. The parameter that disappeared is the point --
//     before:  fold :: <T, A, I: Iterator<T>, F: Fn2<A, T, A>> (...)
//     after:   fold :: <I: Iterator, A, F: Fn2<A, I.Item, A>> (...)
// `&I`, because fold borrows the iterator for the length of the call.
// `ListIter` above takes `ptr<List<T, M>>` because it KEEPS it -- a runtime
// param of a `-> self` def is a field, and rule 1 forbids a `&T` field.
fold :: <I: Iterator, A, F: Fn2<A, I.Item, A>> (it: &I, acc: A, f: F) -> A {
    a := acc
    loop {
        match (it.next()) {
            none    { return a }
            some(v) { a = f(a, v) }
        }
    }
}

// ERASING A SHAPE WITH A TYPE HOLE. A vtable slot returning `Option<self.Item>`
// has no layout until Item is known, so a shape with an unfilled type hole
// cannot be coerced. Fix it at the coercion, with the spelling that already
// means "fill a declared hole":
//
//     it : ptr<Iterator<Item = u8>> := &li
//
// This is NOTE 3's rule -- shape-level generics multiply vtables -- arriving
// at the same place from the other direction, and it needs no new syntax:
// `=` fills a hole, and `to_string<F = hex_u8>` already passes one by name.
// A static bound (`I: Iterator`) needs no such thing, because monomorphising
// knows Item exactly.


// ============================================================================
// std.ord  --  Ord is a POLICY: u8 has no one true ordering, and the
// caller must be able to pick. Nothing is attached to u8. `<`, `<=`, `>` and
// `>=` desugar to it, and `==` and `!=` to Eq.
// ============================================================================

Ord :: <T> -> self {
    compare: (a: T, b: T) -> i32     // no self -> policy
}

// Equality is separate from ordering, because plenty of types have one and not
// the other, and homogeneous-by-default like Add is not: `Eq<A, B>` lets a
// slice compare against a literal without a conversion.
Eq :: <A, B> -> self {
    eq: (a: A, b: B) -> bool
}

// Note what this does NOT replace. `Key<K>` in std.map carries hash AND equals
// in one block, because those two must agree ([P11] in json.z) and because a
// map's key comparison is a choice -- case-folding or not -- that is allowed
// to differ from what `==` means. `Eq` is the default; `Key` is the caller's.

// Anonymous -> in the solver pool. This is what `Ord<u8>` resolves to.
//
// NOTE THE INTRINSICS, because adopting [P17] made them mandatory and the
// first draft of this instance was silently infinitely recursive: `a < b`
// desugars to `Ord<u8>.compare(a, b) < 0`, and inside the pooled `Ord<u8>`
// that is a call to itself. Every primitive instance is where a desugaring
// bottoms out, so every primitive instance is written in instructions. It is
// the same shape of mistake as FRICTION 11 in json.z -- a default instance
// recursing into itself by writing the obvious thing -- and it is worth
// keeping visible.
:: -> Ord<u8> {
    compare = (a, b) -> i32 {
        if (intrinsic_lt_u8(a, b)) { return -1 }
        if (intrinsic_lt_u8(b, a)) { return  1 }
        return 0
    }
}

// Named -> never searched. Reachable only by hand. `Ord<u8>` on the right is
// the pooled instance, not this one -- a type in value position is what the
// solver picks.
reverse_u8 :: -> Ord<u8> {
    // `Ord<u8>` here is the solver's default, NOT this block -- deliberate
    // delegation. `self.compare` would be infinite recursion. Both spellings
    // are needed and they now look different. See NOTE 7.
    compare = (a, b) -> i32 { Ord<u8>.compare(b, a) }
}

// `_` for the allocator: sort does not care which one, and naming a parameter
// only so that `List<T, M>` parses is the noisiest thing in the old draft.
// Every line that used to say `xs.items[j]` now says `xs[j]`, and `sort` no
// longer knows that a List has a pointer in it.
sort :: <T, O: Ord<T> = ?> (xs: &List<T, _>) -> () {
    for (i := 1; i < xs.count; i += 1) {
        v := xs[i]
        j := i
        while (j > 0 && O.compare(xs[j - 1], v) > 0) {
            xs[j] = xs[j - 1]
            j -= 1
        }
        xs[j] = v
    }
}


// ============================================================================
// std.fmt  --  also a POLICY: you want hex and decimal for the same u8.
// ============================================================================

Formattable :: <T> -> self {
    format: (v: T, w: ptr<Writer>) -> ()
}

:: -> Formattable<u8> {
    format = (v, w) -> () {
        if (v >= 10) { self.format(v / 10, w) }   // ME, not the pool. NOTE 7.
        d := '0' + (v % 10)
        w.write_bytes(&d, 1)
    }
}

hex_u8 :: -> Formattable<u8> {
    format = (v, w) -> () {
        digits :: "0123456789abcdef"
        hi := digits[v / 16]
        lo := digits[v % 16]
        w.write_bytes(&hi, 1)
        w.write_bytes(&lo, 1)
    }
}

// A derived instance is an ordinary def: args on the left, result on the
// right. The solver matches the return type. No special impl syntax, no
// pattern on the left-hand side. See NOTE 5.
:: <T, M, F: Formattable<T> = ?> -> Formattable<List<T, M>> {
    format = (v, w) -> () {
        w.write("[")
        for (i := 0; i < v.count; i += 1) {
            if (i > 0) { w.write(", ") }
            F.format(v[i], w)
        }
        w.write("]")
    }
}


// ============================================================================
// std.fmt.str
// ============================================================================

// A Writer, structurally. No closures, no capture, memcpy-movable.
StringWriter :: <M: Allocator> (mem: M) -> self {
    buf := List<u8>(mem)

    write_bytes :: (&self, p: ptr<u8>, n: u32) -> () {
        for (i := 0; i < n; i += 1) { self.buf.push(p[i]) }
    }

    finish :: (&self) -> slice<u8> { self.buf.span() }
}

to_string :: <T, M: Allocator, F: Formattable<T> = ?> (mem: M, v: T) -> slice<u8> {
    b := StringWriter(mem)
    F.format(v, &b)          // &b coerces to ptr<Writer>; the fat pointer is
    b.finish()               // built right here, and only here
}


add_u8 :: (a: u8, v: u8) -> u8 { a + v }


// ============================================================================
// main
// ============================================================================

main :: () -> () {
    mem := Heap()                      // zero-sized
    xs  := List<u8>(mem)               // M = Heap; every alloc call is direct
    defer xs.deinit()

    xs.push(2)
    xs.push(1)

    io.out.write(to_string(mem, xs[0]))              // "2"
    io.out.write(to_string(mem, xs))                 // "[2, 1]"

    // Override the solver by naming the policy. It is a compile-time
    // argument passed by name, so it uses `=`, like any other default.
    io.out.write(to_string<F = hex_u8>(mem, xs[0]))  // "02"
    io.out.write(to_string<F = hex_u8>(mem, xs))     // "[02, 01]"

    sort(&xs)                          // Ord<u8> from the pool
    sort<O = reverse_u8>(&xs)          // by hand

    match (xs.pop()) {
        some (v) { io.out.write(to_string(mem, v)) }
        none     { io.err.write("empty") }
    }

    // Static composition: Tracking<Heap> is a distinct type, calls still direct.
    audited := Tracking(Heap())
    zs := List<u8>(audited)
    defer zs.deinit()

    // Dynamic: the annotation is the coercion. Same List, no second decl,
    // no `dyn`. Now the calls go through a fat pointer.
    arena := Arena(page, 4096)
    boxed : ptr<Allocator> := &arena
    ys := List<u8>(boxed)
    defer ys.deinit()

    // Operators are policy calls, so this is `Add<Vec2, Vec2>.add(...)` and
    // then `Mul<f32, Vec2>.mul(...)`. Neither line needs Vec2 to own anything,
    // and the second one is the case a member could not have expressed.
    v := Vec2(1.0, 2.0) + Vec2(3.0, 4.0)
    w := 2.0 * v
    if (w == Vec2(8.0, 12.0)) { io.out.write("yes") }
    io.out.write(to_string(mem, saturating.add(v, w)))   // the named one, by hand

    // The iterator shape, with its Item supplied by the implementer.
    li    := ListIter(&xs)
    total := fold(&li, 0, add_u8)

    // A closure is what is left over: a block holding `m` with one member
    // named `()`. A closure is a struct; that is the whole point.
    print_u8 :: <M: Allocator> (m: M) (v: u8) -> () { io.out.write(to_string(m, v)) }
    p := print_u8(mem)
    p(7)                               // -> p.()(7)

    // ...and since it is a block with a `()` member, it satisfies Fn<u8, ()>
    // structurally, and erases exactly like every other shape.
    f : ptr<Fn<u8, ()>> := &p
    f(8)
}


// ============================================================================
// NOTE 1 -- shapes vs policies, and why one syntax is enough.
//
// Rust conflates these and pays for it with orphan rules. Go has only shapes.
// Haskell has only policies. You can have both, told apart by one word:
//
//   SHAPE       every hole takes self. An allocator, a writer, a socket --
//               things where the abstraction is a property of the object and
//               there is exactly one sensible answer per type. Satisfied
//               structurally by any type with matching methods, so it needs
//               no registration. Coerce to ptr<Shape> for dynamic dispatch.
//
//   POLICY      no hole takes self. Ord<u8>, Formattable<u8> -- things where
//               several answers exist and the caller must be able to choose. A
//               free-standing value, defaulted by the solver, overridden by
//               name. Works on primitives and foreign types, since nothing has
//               to be attached to them.
//
// The test is not stylistic: can there legitimately be two of these for one
// type? Two orderings of u8, yes -> policy. Two ways for an Arena to
// allocate, no -> shape.
//
// IT ANSWERS QUESTIONS IT WAS NOT DESIGNED FOR, which is the best evidence
// available that it is real rather than tidy. Asked of the operators it says
// `[]` and `()` are members and `+` and `==` are policies -- two ways for a
// List to index itself, no; two ways to add two vectors, yes -- and that split
// is exactly the one the implementation needs, because a policy is not
// attached to a type and so `2.0 * v` needs nobody to own f32.
//
// It is a property of the BLOCK, not of each hole, and it quantifies over
// VALUE holes: a `: type` hole takes no self and is neither shape-like nor
// policy-like, so both kinds of block may carry one (NOTE 9). A block with one
// self value hole and one non-self value hole is an error; it would have no
// dispatch story and no coercion, and admitting it buys nothing.
//
// Anything that must AGREE belongs in ONE block. A hash and an equals solved
// as two independent `= ?` params can be defaulted inconsistently -- a
// case-folding hash with a case-sensitive equals is a silently broken map that
// no type error catches. Grouping is free here, because a policy is just a
// block.
//
// Neither is a new construct. Both are a block with unfilled holes.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE 2 -- static vs dynamic is a type, not a keyword.
//
//     List<u8>(mem)      M = Heap            direct calls, 0-byte field
//     List<u8>(audited)  M = Tracking<Heap>  direct calls, still monomorphic
//     List<u8>(boxed)    M = ptr<Allocator>  fat pointer, indirect calls
//
// One declaration, three behaviors. The cost of the first two: they are
// different types and cannot share an array. That is the trade, and it is now
// visible in the code instead of hidden in a keyword.
//
// The trade gets much worse for RECURSIVE types, which is now the largest
// open problem in the design. See OPEN.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE 3 -- shapes contain holes and nothing else.
//
// An earlier draft let a shape carry provided methods -- `make :: <T> ...`
// with a body, non-virtual, generic, overridable by an implementer. It bought
// `a.make<T>(n)` and cost three things:
//
//   - `:` vs `::` had to be read carefully inside every shape
//   - an implementer could override a provided method, and that override
//     would silently vanish the moment the value was coerced to ptr<Shape>,
//     because non-virtual methods are not in the table
//   - the shape's author owned the convenience layer forever
//
// Moving them to `mem` costs nothing at the call site, because UFCS makes
// `mem.make<T>(capacity)` read the same either way. All three problems go.
//
// What is genuinely lost: a Pool cannot specialize `make` with per-type free
// lists. If that need ever becomes real, the answer is a separate
// `TypedAllocator<T>` shape -- monomorphic holes per instantiation, so the
// specialization is a vtable slot and survives coercion -- not a default.
//
// Background, since the tension is real for anything with a table:
//     GENERIC . OVERRIDABLE . VIRTUAL  --  pick two.
// Shape-level generics escape it by multiplying vtables; that is why
// Iterator<T> is right and Allocator<T> is not.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE 4 -- what lives in the block, and what UFCS may do.
//
// Methods in the block get T, M and the fields in scope for free, and they are
// the only thing shape matching looks at.
//
//     INTRINSIC   declared in the block. Counts for shape satisfaction. May
//                 be virtual. `push`, `alloc`, `write_bytes`.
//     EXTRINSIC   a free function called via UFCS. Convenience only, never
//                 satisfies a shape. `make`, `drop`, `write`.
//
// The second half of that rule is load-bearing, and follows directly from UFCS
// resolving the free function in the CALLER's scope. If UFCS could satisfy a
// shape, then "does Heap satisfy Allocator" would depend on what is imported,
// and two modules could coerce the same value and build different vtables.
//
// With that split you do get extension methods -- add anything to a type you
// do not own, it just cannot participate in a shape.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE 5 -- derived instances are ordinary defs.
//
// The pattern-on-the-left form
//     Formattable<List<T,M>> :: <T, M, F: Formattable<T> = ?> { ... }
// was a second, unification-shaped mechanism. Writing it as an anonymous def
// with a return type makes it an ordinary declaration; the solver matches the
// return type, which it already had to do for `= ?` params.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE 6 -- why `operator` had to go, and why half the operators left with it.
//
// It was the language's only two-token name, and the only place the word
// appeared. Naming the member `[]` costs nothing and removes a keyword.
//
// THE OTHER HALF OF THE OPERATORS ARE NOT MEMBERS AT ALL. Written as one,
// arithmetic breaks on the second line of vector code:
//
//     v * 2.0      ->  a member of Vec2 named `*`.  Fine.
//     2.0 * v      ->  a member of f32 named `*`.   Nobody owns f32.
//
// UFCS looks like the rescue -- `*(2.0, v)` resolves a free function in the
// caller's scope -- and it is the rescue NOTE 4 forbids, correctly: if it were
// allowed, "is Vec2 numeric" would depend on what happened to be imported.
// So a member cannot express scaling, and no bound could ever require
// arithmetic of a type parameter.
//
// The test in NOTE 1 says why, and says what to do instead. Two ways to add
// two vectors -- saturating and wrapping -- so addition is a CHOICE, and a
// choice is a policy: `a + b` is `Add<A, B>.add(a, b)`, solver-found, with the
// operand types as the search key. Everything awkward goes at once. Scalars on
// the left work, because a policy is attached to nothing. Primitives stop
// being special: `Add<u8, u8>` is a pooled instance like any other and the
// language has no built-in arithmetic, only built-in instructions inside those
// instances. `Ord` and `Eq` were already policies, so `<` and `==` land where
// std had already put them. And `lerp :: <T> (a: T, b: T, t: f32) -> T` --
// generic code over anything numeric -- becomes sayable, which under the
// member reading it was not, in any form.
//
// What it costs: a fixed precedence table, since a member name carries none.
// Not user-extensible, which is the right call and is also true of every
// language that has tried the alternative.
//
// The member half pays for itself twice, because the same move names the
// member `()`, and that is what a closure is.
// `print_u8 :: <M: Allocator> (m: M) (v: u8)` desugars to
//
//     print_u8 :: <M: Allocator> (m: M) -> self { (): (&self, v: u8) -> () {...} }
//
// -- an ordinary `-> self` block, whose runtime params are its captured fields,
// exactly as everywhere else. Two questions that were open in the previous
// draft answer themselves: `print_u8(mem)` has its OWN type (so it is free),
// and it becomes a shared type precisely when you write `ptr<Fn<u8, ()>>`,
// which is the same coercion as `ptr<Allocator>` and not a second mechanism.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE 7 -- how an instance names itself, and why `self` and not its own name.
//
// A policy is chosen at the CALL SITE, so when an instance's body recurses
// it has to name a policy again -- and naming the type gets the solver's
// default, not the instance the caller picked. Written the obvious way, a
// `pretty` printer prints the outermost value pretty and every child with the
// default, silently, one level down.
//
// A NAMED instance can just say its own name (`pretty.format(...)`), which is
// ordinary recursion and needs nothing new. Two things push past that:
//
//   - The anonymous instances -- the ones in the pool, which is to say the
//     DEFAULT for every type -- have no name to say by construction. Naming an
//     instance is exactly what removes it from the pool.
//
//   - A parameterised instance must re-thread its own compile-time arguments
//     by hand. `pretty :: <indent: u32 = 2> -> Formattable<Json>` has to write
//     `pretty<indent>.format(...)`; writing `pretty.format(...)` silently
//     resets every nested level to the default while the top level uses what
//     the caller asked for. `self` carries the arguments and cannot be got
//     wrong.
//
// Both spellings remain necessary, and they now read differently, which is the
// real win:
//
//     self.format(child, w)              the instance I am      -- recursion
//     Formattable<Json>.format(v, w)     the solver's default   -- delegation
//
// `reverse_u8` wants the second on purpose. A pretty-printer wants the first.
// Before this, only the second was sayable and the two were the same text.
//
// This is not a second meaning for `self`. See KEYWORDS: the receiver exists
// to say which instance, and an instance is already a single value.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE 8 -- coherence, and why the pool is global and unique.
//
// The rule is in the header: one anonymous instance per (policy, type), pool
// is whole-program, a duplicate or an overlap is an error at the declarations.
// The alternatives were:
//
//   IMPORT-SCOPED POOL. Rejected on the same grounds as NOTE 4. A type that
//   defaults a policy parameter -- `Map<slice<u8>, V>` picking its `Key` --
//   would have a different layout in two modules that imported differently,
//   with one spelling. The language already refuses to let shape satisfaction
//   depend on imports; it cannot then let type identity depend on them.
//
//   ERROR AT THE USE SITE instead of the declaration. Attractive, because a
//   conflict nobody exercises would be harmless. It is a dead end: the fix at
//   a use site is to name the instance you meant, and the instances in
//   conflict are ANONYMOUS -- they have no name by construction. Ambiguity you
//   cannot spell your way out of is worse than a build error.
//
//   MOST-SPECIFIC-WINS, so that `Formattable<List<u8, M>>` could override
//   `Formattable<List<T, M>>` for strings. Rejected: it makes the solver's
//   answer depend on a subtyping order among patterns, which is where every
//   language that has tried it spends the next decade. Overlap is an error.
//   Wanting the special case means wanting a distinct type or a named
//   instance, and both are one line.
//
//   AN ORPHAN RULE, checked. Rejected because extrinsic instances on foreign
//   types is the ENTIRE advantage policies hold over traits -- it is why `Ord`
//   works on u8 and why the fmt derivation in this file can be written by
//   someone who owns neither Formattable nor List. Keeping the check would
//   have bought coherence-by-construction at the price of the feature.
//
// So the orphan rule survives as a convention rather than a check: an
// anonymous instance is a claim on the global default, so publish one only for
// a policy or a type you own. Where two libraries both claim one, the program
// does not build until one names its instance. That is a real cost and it is
// the deliberate trade: a loud failure in a rare case, in exchange for
// extrinsic instances in every common one.
//
// [P17] raises the stakes and does not change the answer. With arithmetic
// solver-dispatched, a duplicate `Add<Vec2, Vec2>` is a question about what
// `+` means in every expression rather than about a sort order -- which is an
// argument for deciding it, and this is the decision.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE 9 -- a type hole, and why it is not a parameter.
//
// `Item: type` in a shape, `Out: type` in an operator policy. Both are the
// thing other languages call an associated type, and here it is not a new
// construct: `type` is a type whose values are types, a hole is a member you
// did not supply, so a hole of type `type` is a type the implementer supplies.
// `Item :: T` fills it, at zero bytes, like every other `::` member.
//
// THE LINE BETWEEN A PARAMETER AND A HOLE IS WHO DECIDES, and it is mechanical
// once stated: the solver searches on parameters, so anything the caller must
// know before an instance can be found is a parameter, and anything the
// instance itself determines is a hole. `Mul<Vec2, f32>` searches on both
// operand types; the result type is not a search key and could not be one --
// you would have to know the answer to look it up.
//
// What it removes, from code that was already written:
//     json.z   fold :: <T, A, I: Iterator<T>, F: Fn2<A, T, A>>
//     here     fold :: <I: Iterator, A, F: Fn2<A, I.Item, A>>
// `T` was never a choice a caller of `fold` made; it belonged to the iterator,
// and had to be spelled because the shape had nowhere to put it.
//
// Two rules come with it, both already stated above and both consequences
// rather than additions: the shape/policy test quantifies over value holes
// only, and a shape with an unfilled type hole cannot be coerced to
// `ptr<Shape>` -- fix it at the coercion, `ptr<Iterator<Item = u8>>`, which is
// the existing "fill a hole by name with `=`" and nothing more.
//
// ============================================================================
// CHANGED IN THIS REVISION -- all of it found by writing src/ecs.z, an
// entity-component-system and the game loop around it, chosen because it
// MUTATES and STORES where json.z only built and printed. That file keeps the
// full log.
//
//   ADDED    OPERATORS ARE POLICIES, except `[]` and `()`, which take self and
//            stay members. `a + b` is `Add<A, B>.add(a, b)`. Found by `2.0 * v`
//            being unwritable as a member and unrescuable by UFCS without
//            breaking NOTE 4. Brings std.num, `Eq`, and NOTE 6's rewrite.
//   ADDED    A HOLE MAY BE A TYPE -- `Item: type`, `Out: type`. An associated
//            type, with no new construct: a hole is a member you did not
//            supply. Brings std.iter, NOTE 9, and the rule that a shape with an
//            unfilled type hole cannot be erased without fixing it
//            (`ptr<Iterator<Item = u8>>`). Required by the operator policies,
//            since a result type must not be a search key.
//   ADDED    COHERENCE. One anonymous instance per (policy, type), the pool is
//            whole-program, duplicates and overlaps are errors at the
//            declarations, and there is no orphan rule -- the convention
//            replaces the check. NOTE 8. This closes the oldest item in the
//            file, and the operator change is what forced it: a second
//            anonymous `Add<Vec2, Vec2>` is a question about `+` everywhere.
//   ADDED    ORDER INDEPENDENCE, relied on since the first draft and never
//            stated. Recursive types, the solver and coherence all require it.
//   ADDED    COMPILE-TIME VALUE ARGUMENTS as the third kind, `<indent: u32 = 2>`.
//            Used by `List`'s `capacity :: 10` and by every array size, and
//            never mentioned. Mixing one with an inferred type parameter is
//            what makes named passing the only way to call a declaration.
//   ADDED    `.*` IS THE DEREF, replacing postfix `^`, which was also xor in
//            two hash functions in this repo and would have had to parse
//            `h^ ^ x`. It is not an arbitrary swap: `.` reaches through a
//            pointer and stops at a member, `.*` reaches through and stops at
//            the value. The three implicit reach-throughs are now written down
//            as well, having been used since the first draft and never stated.
//   ADDED    A PARAMETER MAY BE A PLACE -- `(out: &u32)` -- which is `&self`
//            without the special case. It cannot be stored, because rule 1
//            forbids a `&T` field, so `(w: &World)` says "I will not keep
//            this" and `(w: ptr<World>)` says "I might". The first thing in
//            the language able to say that.
//   ADDED    PLACES. `-> &T` is a sixth projection: a LOCATION escapes. It is
//            what makes `xs[i] = v` writable for a user container, and what
//            makes `-> {a, b}` usable on one -- `List` now exports six names
//            and hides its storage, which was unwritable before. Four rules,
//            no new pointer type, nothing in the ABI. [P22] in ecs.z, adopted
//            in a third form: neither a pointer return nor a paired `[]=`.
//   DECIDED  NO AUTOMATIC DESTRUCTION. A block does not get a destructor that
//            runs at scope exit. `deinit` is an ordinary member and `defer` is
//            how you call it, which is the same idea written where it can be
//            seen. The alternative is not one feature: once destructors fire
//            on their own, the language must also define what happens on
//            assignment over a live value, on return, and on copy, and that is
//            the road to move semantics and a borrow checker. This language's
//            claim is that cost is visible and there is no hidden machinery;
//            a destructor at a closing brace is hidden machinery. The price,
//            accepted with open eyes: containers do not free their elements,
//            an erased value in a container is kept alive by hand, and both
//            are leaks the compiler will not mention.
//   ADDED    A VALUE MAY NOT BE DROPPED SILENTLY; `_ := xs.pop()` says so out
//            loud. The fourth use of `_`, and the one an error channel needs.
//   CHANGED  the shape/policy test quantifies over VALUE holes only, so a type
//            hole may appear in either kind of block.
//   ADDED    NOTE 8 (coherence) and NOTE 9 (type holes); NOTE 1 and NOTE 6
//            rewritten.
//
// PREVIOUS REVISION -- found by writing src/json.z, a JSON parser and the
// containers under it: `-> oneof` and holes-quantified-by-the-projection,
// `match`, `_`, `try`, `defer`, `-> {a, b}` as visibility, the instantiation
// rule, a shape in `<>` is static and in `()` is erased, `<F = hex_u8>` over
// `<F: hex_u8>`, `[]` over `operator []`, `List.pop` returning an Option,
// shape-vs-policy as a property of the block, DICTIONARY renamed to POLICY,
// and `self` available lexically in a block with no runtime group.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// OPEN
//
//   - MEMORY. Two of the six are now decided -- places, above, and no
//     automatic destruction -- and the rest are what is left. They are still
//     one question: the language can say what a value IS and cannot yet say
//     WHERE IT LIVES, WHO MAY WRITE IT, or WHEN IT DIES.
//
//       DO VALUES MOVE?  Nothing says whether returning a block copies it or
//       constructs it in place, so `stores.push(&self.pos)` -- a structure
//       pointing into itself -- is unanswerable rather than wrong. The cheapest
//       answer consistent with everything else here is that a value has one
//       address for its lifetime, and it has to be WRITTEN DOWN: places,
//       closures stored in data, and `ptr<Shape>` in a container all depend on
//       it. It is the next thing to decide, because the constructor body
//       depends on it.
//
//       THE CONSTRUCTOR BODY.  A `-> self` block has field initialisers and no
//       statements, so anything needing `self`, or two fields at once, or a
//       step that can fail, has nowhere to go. `-> Result<self, E>` was adopted
//       for the fallible case and is the wrong half: it gave the constructor a
//       return type and still no body. The cost is two-phase construction,
//       unchecked -- ecs.z's `w.wire()`.
//
//       OWNED ERASURE.  `ptr<Shape>` is a borrow with no owner, which is right
//       for an argument and wrong for a container: a schedule of `ptr<System>`
//       holds pointers into locals, and declaration order in main is
//       load-bearing and unenforced. A `Box<Shape, M>` would fix it and cannot
//       exist without a destructor, so with destruction decided this one is
//       decided too: you keep the values alive yourself.
//
//       WHO MAY WRITE.  `::`/`:=` encode immutable/mutable for BINDINGS and
//       nothing does for POINTEES, so a `ptr<T>` is an unrestricted handle of
//       indefinite life. In a simulation this stops being a documentation
//       problem: a pointer into a store is invalidated by any push, and an
//       event emitted mid-iteration reenters the loop that emitted it. Places
//       make an interior pointer easier to take, which raises the stakes
//       without changing the kind of hole it is. This is the one cost in the
//       language not visible in a type, and the only one of the six with no
//       cheap answer.
//
//   - THE DISCRIMINANT IS NOT A VALUE. Both programs hit it. json.z wanted
//     `expect(Token.colon())` to mean "same variant, ignore the payload"; ecs.z
//     wanted an ordinal per variant, which is what an archetype bitmask and a
//     save file are made of. Wanted: `S.tag`, a derived sum of unit variants,
//     with `tag_of(v)` total, comparable, and ordered.
//
//   - NO `for (x := it)`. `Iterator` is declared by the standard library and
//     used by neither program written against it, because the `loop`/`match`/
//     `Option` consumer is four lines to replace one. That is a signal about
//     the sugar, not about the shape.
//
//   - ARITY FAMILIES. `Fn`, `Fn2`, `Fn3` were "cheap enough to live with" when
//     they were an implementation detail. `Join2`, `Join3`, `Join4` at a query
//     interface are public surface. Variadic compile-time args are the only
//     real answer and nothing cheaper has been found.
//
//   - STRUCTURAL MATCHING HAS NO ADAPTER. A foreign type with the right
//     operations under the wrong names cannot be made to satisfy a shape
//     except by a wrapper costing a pointer, a vtable and a name. Go has the
//     same hole; it is written down rather than solved.
//
//   - IS `ptr<Shape>` COMPARABLE? It is two words built at the coercion site.
//     Whether two coercions of one block are equal decides whether an observer
//     list can ever remove a subscriber, and nothing says.
//
//   - `_` DOES NOT REACH PROJECTION POSITION, so it fixes the consumers of a
//     plumbing parameter and not the wrappers: `iter` must name the type it
//     declined to name in order to spell its own return. Two `_` are also two
//     independent parameters, so it cannot say "the same type I did not name".
//
//   - MONOMORPHISATION IS VIRAL THROUGH CONTAINING TYPES. Livable, and the
//     answer is an alias collapsing the parameter once in the application --
//     `Doc :: Json<ptr<Arena>>`, `A :: ptr<Arena>`. Two unrelated programs,
//     same one-line fix, so it is a pattern rather than a coincidence. The
//     residual cost is ONE ALLOCATOR PER TYPE, and it does not reach a library
//     shape: `System<M>` stays generic because a library cannot name the
//     application's allocator.
//
//   - FORWARD DECLARATIONS. Bodiless means hole, so a hand-written header full
//     of `func insert_item<T>(...);` declares an abstract module rather than a
//     module. Headers have to be generated from implementations.
//
//   - Does `capacity :: 10` make List-with-cap-10 a different type from
//     cap-20? Zig says yes.
//
//   - Is the ptr<Shape> coercion implicit at call sites (`F.format(v, &b)`) or
//     does it need the annotation, as `boxed` does? Implicit is nicer to write
//     and hides a representation change from direct calls to indirect ones.
// ============================================================================
